# Package update @VERSION@ (signed repo @REPO@): signed templates, confirmed POA&M numbers, two alert fixes. No reinstall.

Internal test release for the CyberHygiene Project development team. Not an official Rocky Linux product (`NOTICE.txt`).

This update is packages only. It needs no new ISO and changes no install step. It is for sites that run 0.7.0.

## What changed

- **`chp-templates` is now a signed release.** In 0.7.0 the templates package (1.4.0-3) was a lab candidate: it was
  checked by its fingerprint only. 1.4.0-4 is built from the signed 1.4.0 release, so its source has the project key's
  signature.
- **The POA&M numbers are confirmed.** The NIST requirement numbers on the document server's seed rows T-08 to T-16 were
  marked "pending owner confirmation" in 0.7.0. The owner confirmed them on 2026-11-27 and the notice is gone. The
  numbers themselves did not change. A site that copied the rows already has the right ones.
- **The nightly document check no longer raises a false alert.** On a new 0.7.0 server the first nightly backup added
  the line `'maintenance' => false` to NextCloud's `config.php`, and every 03:10 check after that alerted that the file
  "does not match its fapolicyd trust entry". The check now accepts exactly that one added line. Any other change to the
  file is still reported. You have nothing to run: the next check clears it. If you already re-ran
  `chp-site documents enable` or `chp-site documents trust`, nothing changes for you.
- **The ISSO override alert always says who and which document.** When an ISSO overrides the approval rule and gives a
  very long reason (about 2,000 bytes), the level-12 alert used to show raw placeholders (`$(dstuser)`, `$(path)`)
  instead of the name and the file. The alert did fire. Now the alert carries the first 400 bytes of the reason and ends
  with `...[truncated; full text in the ledger]`, so the person and the document are always named. The ledger still
  keeps the whole reason. (Known issue P3-D16.)

## Apply it

1. **Check this folder** as `0.7.0/VERIFY.md` describes: `SHA256SUMS.asc` against the project key, then
   `shasum -a 256 -c SHA256SUMS`.
2. **Every host:** unpack the repo over the local repo as root and upgrade. The `--refresh` matters.

       rm -rf /var/lib/chp/repo/*
       tar -xzf chp-repo-@REPO@.tar.gz -C /var/lib/chp/repo --strip-components=1
       dnf --refresh --enablerepo=chp upgrade

3. **The site-stick kit** for this update (`site-stick-kit-@VERSION@/`) holds the corrected POA&M seed rows file. If your
   site copied the rows into its `poam.md`, there is nothing to change; use the new file for the next site.

## Expect one AIDE alert, then accept it

The next daily check reports the update. Most entries show only a new inode, because the package files were rewritten.
Expected changes:
- **changed, every host:** `/usr/bin/chp-site`, `/usr/share/chp-site/chp-site.pyz`,
  `/usr/share/licenses/chp-site/LICENSE`, and `RPM-GPG-KEY-cyberhygiene`, `RPM-GPG-KEY-EPEL-9` and `RPM-GPG-KEY-WAZUH`
  in `/etc/pki/rpm-gpg/`.
- **changed, where `chp-templates` is installed** (workstations; a server only if you installed it): the files under
  `/usr/share/chp-templates/` (more than 100) and `/usr/share/licenses/chp-templates/LICENSE`.
- Nothing is added or removed.

Anything outside this list was not made by this update: investigate it before accepting. Accept as the ISSO or a named
delegate:

    chp-site aide-accept --approver "Your Name" --reason "@VERSION@: signed templates, alert fixes"

## Known limits

- The server's OpenSCAP tailoring file is still not applied by the installer's kickstart. That waits for the next ISO.
- The other 0.7.0 known issues stand (`docs/release/0.7.0-known-issues.md`).
- **Proved on VMs only.**
